  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Thailand faces persistent decent work challenges despite its upper-middle-income status, including informality, labour migration, social protection gaps, demographic ageing, skills mismatches, and climate-related risks. The Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP) aligns with Thailand&#x2019;s national development strategies and the UNSDCF to promote inclusive and sustainable growth, reduce inequalities, and strengthen labour market institutions, with particular attention to youth, women, migrant workers and workers in the informal economy.

In this context, the programme places strategic emphasis on strengthening the institutional, technical and representational capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations to ensure they are strong, representative and influential actors in social dialogue and labour market governance. Support will focus on organizational development, leadership and policy analysis capacity, and enhanced outreach to workers in non-standard and informal employment, including migrant workers. By reinforcing their ability to engage effectively in tripartite mechanisms and national policy processes, workers&#x2019; organizations will be better positioned to advocate for labour rights, expanded social protection, safer workplaces and a just transition to a more sustainable economy. These efforts contribute to more inclusive governance of labour markets and the progressive realization of decent work for all in Thailand.</narrative>
  </description>
